A||||8||SLYGUY||Spitting Fuel!!!||||8||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||10-08-2001||12:11 AM||||Before I changed the plugs, cap and rotor my GT was spitting fuel out the exhaust at all rpm. Changing the items above made it run much better, we won't talk about how miserable the cap/rotor and plugs were!!<P>The car doesn't spit fuel at idle (nice idle by the way), but will if you rap the throttle, and at cold startup. Black/grey smoke momentarily when I run the motor up to operating temperature and let it sit approx. 20 minutes and go out there and start her up.<BR>Small amount of fuel and smoke at cold startup.<P>I'm gonna tackle the fuel filter, and pressure regulator next and also run a couple bottles of fuel injector cleaner through it. <BR>What do you guys think?<P>Sly||||reg||8
Z||000001||Bluebottlejunky||10-08-2001||12:19 AM||||Hmm, Sounds like leaky fuel injectors, the new plugs...just helped burn the fuel faster..so intern dosen't spit as much fuel..how were the old plugs..were they gasy? black?<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: <B>KilleRedGT</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||10-08-2001||12:23 AM||||The plugs looked like hell. <BR>They were the wrong temp code also.<P>What is the best cure for leaky injectors? Will a injector tick when it is faulty/leaking? Got a ticking that sounds like a relay quickly turning off and on.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||10-08-2001||11:29 AM||||bumpety bump bump||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-08-2001||08:18 PM||||There is no posible way to pump raw liquid fuel out the exhaust of a running car. The exhaust of even a poorly running car is too hot to ever allow it. Liquid out the exhaust may smell like raw fuel but it's water.<P>From what your describing, it sounds like you may have a leaking head gasket. <P>A compression check should be done. Be careful to do each cylinder exactly the same way. Don't forget to kill both the ignition and injectors. block the throttle at WOT.<P>An exhaust gas analyzer can be held over an open thermostat neck to detect exhaust fumes in the cooling system. (Don't immerse the analyzer probe.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||SLYGUY||12-13-2001||03:27 AM||||<BR>You replied:<BR>"Liquid out the exhaust may smell like raw fuel but it's water."<BR> <BR>So, the question I ask of you is this:<BR> <BR>Why was the apparent water spitting much worse before I replaced the plugs, cap and rotor, EGR, and O2 sensors?<BR>The car runs very well and has had no erratic temperature fluctuations. No oil in coolant, coolant in oil either.<BR>The car has done this for over five thousand miles now.<BR> <BR>Your insight is greatly appreciated.<BR> <BR>Please respond both here and at:<BR>slyguy@webuniverse.net<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg">   <P>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 12-13-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||12-14-2001||03:19 AM||||Bumpety Bump Bump  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ProudGT||12-14-2001||12:30 PM||||Simple Test. Propane Torch in the exhaust stream. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Turbo88||12-14-2001||01:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B> <BR>Why was the apparent water spitting much worse before I replaced the plugs, cap and rotor, EGR, and O2 sensors?<BR>The car runs very well and has had no erratic temperature fluctuations. No oil in coolant, coolant in oil either.<BR>The car has done this for over five thousand miles now.<BR> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>One reason that would cause it to expel less water is that, with your better ignition system inplace, it is burning the fuel air mixture more completely and vaporizing more of the water. Perhaps it is allowing the water to condense inside the muffler which is why you are seeing drops out of the pipes. Check your antifreese to see if it looks normal. Can you sense a "sweet" smell in your exhaust? Pressure checking the engine would be a good indicator of how well your engine is sealed. <P>Good Luck..<P>||||reg||1
